<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Arbor runs wasm extensions, and wasmtime compiles them to native code at load time.
    Under the hardened runtime — which the macOS bundler turns on when it signs — executing
    from a page the kernel has no signature for is a SIGKILL, not an error: the process dies
    with `CODESIGNING / Invalid Page` the first time it jumps into compiled guest code, and
    nothing in Arbor gets the chance to say why.

    These two are what every JIT on macOS declares. `allow-jit` covers MAP_JIT regions;
    `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` covers the plain anonymous mapping wasmtime uses, and
    is the one that actually keeps a mesh-source extension from taking the whole app down.

    They do weaken the hardened runtime for this process, and that is the trade: an app that
    runs a JIT cannot also promise every executable page was signed ahead of time. What keeps
    a guest contained is `arbor-plugin-wasm`'s capability gate — no ambient WASI, every effect
    an explicit host function — not the page protections.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
